# Editor Document Data Placement

Both `currentData` and `metadata` on an `EditorDocument` are persisted to the user's localStorage. Misplacing data into either channel wastes storage, degrades performance, and can leak stale state across sessions.

## Channel rules

| Channel | Purpose | OK to store | NOT OK to store |
|---------|---------|-------------|-----------------|
| `data.original` / `data.current` | Dirty-state tracking, session restoration | File content (BPMN XML, JSON) | Parsed models, fetched lists, computed state |
| `metadata` | View-related restoration state | Scroll position, zoom level, active tab index, revision counters | Selections, parsed models, data lists, fetched records |
| Private model fields + public getters | Working/production data | Selections, parsed models, data lists, fetched records, computed state | — |

## Pane data access pattern (Debugger pattern)

Panes access model data by casting `props.editorDocumentModel` to the concrete model type and calling its public getters:

```typescript
const model = props.editorDocumentModel as MyDocumentModel | null;
const items = model?.getItems() ?? [];
```

NEVER use `studio.getSharedRessource()` for per-document data. `registerSharedRessource` is a global singleton store — two tabs of the same type overwrite each other's state.

## Selection re-render trigger

When the model's selection changes, increment a private `selectionRevision` counter and write it to metadata. This triggers the Workbench's metadata-change event, causing panes to re-render and re-read from the model's getter. The actual selection object is never persisted.

```typescript
this.selectionRevision++;
this.updateMetadata({ selectionRevision: this.selectionRevision });
```

